{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The independent Auto-Train Corporation (1971-1981), a privately owned passenger railroad that pioneered the Lorton-Sanford car-carrying route Amtrak's Auto Train still runs. Route geometry is taken from the modern Amtrak route's own GTFS-sourced alignment for this corridor (see amtrak.geojson), since the historical train ran the same physical line; where the historical train ran beyond or differently from today's Amtrak route, that is described in the segment notes. Era boundaries follow real, sourced milestones; headway_min and avg_speed_kmh within each era are estimates, not from real timetables.", "source": "Wikipedia (Auto-Train Corporation), american-rails.com", "pulled": "20260817", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Auto-Train Corporation", "line": "Auto-Train Corporation: Lorton, VA \u2013 Sanford, FL", "id": "auto-train-corp", "color": "#d00000", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 70, "speed_source": "estimated/blended across the line's history; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1971, "end": 1981, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "The Auto-Train Corporation was a privately owned passenger railroad - a rarity in the Amtrak era - running its own rolling stock over leased rails, with autorack cars so passengers could bring their cars along. Service began Dec 6 1971 between Lorton, VA and Sanford, FL behind 17 new GE U36B locomotives. Popular with travelers, but operating costs outran revenue and the last trains ran in April 1981. Amtrak acquired the Lorton and Sanford terminals and much of the rolling stock, and runs the Auto Train over the same route today - see amtrak.geojson. estimated; frequency not sourced from a real timetable", "sources": ["Segment-level: this segment's own note carries the citation for its dates and service. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Auto-Train Corporation), american-rails.com"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-81.50998, 29.37411], "to": [-81.45873, 29.21691], "chord_mi": 11.23, "traced_mi": 11.34, "snap_m": [15, 17], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Sanford Subdivision (11.34 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-81.68526, 29.98424], "to": [-81.61868, 29.84012], "chord_mi": 10.62, "traced_mi": 10.75, "snap_m": [20, 50], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["Sanford Subdivision (10.75 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-81.96004, 31.31849], "to": [-82.02766, 31.00847], "chord_mi": 21.77, "traced_mi": 21.86, "snap_m": [84, 20], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["CSX Nahunta Subdivision (21.86 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-81.94327, 30.72363], "to": [-81.84077, 30.57657], "chord_mi": 11.65, "traced_mi": 11.84, "snap_m": [26, 10], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["CSX Nahunta Subdivision (11.84 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-82.02766, 31.00847], "to": [-82.01755, 30.86302], "chord_mi": 10.07, "traced_mi": 10.06, "snap_m": [20, 13], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["CSX Nahunta Subdivision (10.06 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-81.88792, 31.59943], "to": [-81.96004, 31